 "drivingQuestion": "What practical difference does Rami bar Avin's definition make?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "the definition bears on sales",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.20b.15",
   "daf": "20b",
   "sentenceId": 14,
   "he": "אָמַר רָמֵי בַּר אָבִין: עִטְרָנָא — פְּסוּלְתָּא דְזִיפְתָּא. שַׁעֲוָה — פְּסוּלְתָּא דְּדוּבְשָׁא.",
   "en": "«Rami bar Avin said: Tar [itran]» is the «by-product of pitch.» When wood is burned to extract pitch, a clearer liquid oozes out after the pitch, and that is tar. Similarly, «wax» is the «by-product of honey.»"
  },
  {
   "id": "shabbat.21a.1",
   "daf": "21a",
   "sentenceId": 0,
   "he": "לְמַאי נָפְקָא מִינַּהּ? לְמִקָּח וּמִמְכָּר.",
   "en": "The Gemara asks: «What» is the «practical difference» that emerges from that which Rami bar Avin taught? The Gemara explains: Its significance is with regard «to buying and selling.» One who buys tar can insist upon receiving the by-product of pitch and no other material. The same is true with regard to wax and honey."
  }
 ],
